grizzly-gate-report/

# Vendored semgrep rules (gh#320): reproduced from the pinned image by
# `just vendor-semgrep`, hashed by `just check-vendored`, read by
# `just scan-semgrep`. Git-ignored because the tree carries semgrep test
# fixtures that are not JSON, which the pre-commit duplicate-key guard
# refuses to commit. Never hand-edit; re-vendor instead.
gate-semgrep-rules/

# Frontend
web/node_modules
web/dist/

# grizzly-gate local toolchain install dir
web/.grizzly-gate.node_modules/
.pr-body.md
__pycache__/

# Host-path contamination (AGENTS.md "Tool output never goes to a host path").
# A host path whose leading `/` is lost lands in the tree as a top-level
# `home/…`, `.tmp/…` or `.paperclip/…` directory. These entries are anchored at
# the worktree root and cover only that shape. Coverage limit: the top-level
# entries `home/`, `.tmp/` and `.paperclip/` and nothing else — a top-level
# `tmp/` or `paperclip/` dump (no leading dot) is NOT ignored. No bare
# basename pattern is used here: a basename entry hides its own literal name at
# any depth, so `.tmp/` hides `.a/.tmp/` and `tmp/` hides `.a/tmp/`, either of
# which could be a real directory (gh#346). No depth-threshold scrub either —
# it misses shallow paths. These entries do not prevent contamination; the
# AGENTS.md clause is the control. They only stop the known shape from being
# swept into a commit by `git add -A`/`commit -a` — and they hide the shape
# from `git status`, which is why that clause, not this file, is what you
# actually rely on. Each name is probeable:
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v home/flinn/.tmp/x.json   # /home/
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v .tmp/x.json              # /.tmp/
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v .paperclip/x.json        # /.paperclip/
/home/
/.tmp/
/.paperclip/
